<title>ITALY: Reuters Asset Allocation (Italy) - June 6.</title>
<headline>Reuters Asset Allocation (Italy) - June 6.</headline>
<dateline>MILAN 1997-06-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Italian banks and funds have reduced their weight on Italy's bond and stock markets for this month, putting more funds into foreign markets, a Reuters Asset Allocation Poll for June showed on Friday.</p>
<p>Analysts noted the reduced exposure to Italy reflects growing uncertainty over the future of European monetary union after an election victory for French Socialists and a row between the German government and the Bundesbank over the revaluation of some of the central bank's assets.</p>
<p>&quot;The market is discounting the convergence process and personally I believe (convergence) will continue. But since this is already in the prices, the market is more vulnerable to recoils,&quot; Alessandro Fugnoli, strategist at Caboto, said.</p>
<p>Fugnoli said he continued to be overweight on Italy even though he was slightly less optimistic than a month ago.</p>
<p>On average analysts cut more weight from Italian equities than from bonds.</p>
<p>&quot;In the short run, bonds always offer that extra certainty of yields that the equities market cannot offer,&quot; said one analyst who asked not to be named. The analyst said he had reduced the weight on long term Italian bonds and brought it over to floating rate notes (CCT).</p>
<p>Scope for further gains on the Milan stock market were seen as limited.</p>
<p>&quot;Only slight improvements in company revenue are in sight at the moment, interest rates are not going to fall at least until September because the inflation outlook is no longer so positive, and finally new privatisations on the market absorb liquidity which would otherwise have gone to the equity market,&quot; the analyst said.</p>
<p>Banco di Sardegna diminished its weight on Italian equities slightly, saying it considered the current levels for the Mibtel index to be appropriate and did not see scope for much improvement. The Mibtel index opened 0.17 percent up at 12,277 on Friday.</p>
<p>&quot;This is why we would rather shift to foreign equities, in particular to the U.S., which is sort of like a safehaven, and emerging markets, like the former Soviet bloc and Turkey, which can offer something special to the portfolio,&quot; said Barbara Giani, analyst at Banco di Sardegna.</p>
<p>Reuters collected the allocation figures from nine Italian banks and investment funds from June 2 to June 4. The next poll will be published at the beginning of July.</p>
